<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Okello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–c. 1971</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Okello was a Ugandan-born labourer and revolutionary who led the armed rising of 12 January 1964 that overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar. For a short time he styled himself field marshal and broadcast threats over the radio, but he was soon sidelined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-shirazi-party">Afro-Shirazi Party</a> leaders and expelled from Zanzibar. He disappeared in about 1971.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Gideon Okello was born on 26 October 1937 in Lango District in the Uganda Protectorate and baptised at the age of two. Orphaned at eleven, he left his relatives at fifteen and drifted through Uganda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and Tanganyika as a clerk, manservant, gardener and odd-job man before training as a bricklayer. Arrested in Nairobi on allegations of rape, he spent two years in prison, an experience that left him with an intense hatred of the British.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1959 Okello went to Pemba and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-shirazi-party">Afro-Shirazi Party</a> of Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeid-karume">Abeid Karume</a>, which opposed the dominance of the Arab minority. His speeches against British rule, the South Asian merchants and the Arab ruling class won him a following, and the rigged victory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanzibar-nationalist-party">Zanzibar Nationalist Party</a> in 1961 convinced him that only violent revolution would bring the African majority to power. In 1963 he moved to Zanzibar, worked as a house painter and used his travels to build a small army bound by strict rules: no sex, no raw meat and no alcohol.</p><p>Okello said that God had ordered him in dreams to break the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. On the night before the rising he told his men to kill all Arabs between 18 and 25 years of age, sparing pregnant and elderly women. His poorly armed fighters surprised the police in Stone Town, and he announced over the radio that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> must kill his family and himself, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshid-bin-abdullah">Jamshid bin Abdullah</a> had escaped and later reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Between 18 and 20 January between 2,000 and 4,000 Arabs, South Asians and Comorians were killed, thousands of rapes were committed, and within weeks a fifth of the population had died or fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Karume, made president, and Abdulrahman Muhammad Babu kept Okello at a distance. His Freedom Military Force was largely disarmed by March, and he was refused entry on returning from the mainland and formally removed as Field Marshal on 11 March. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> and Uganda, was imprisoned several times, and was last seen with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> in 1971. The former diplomat Don Petterson wrote that most observers believed Amin had arranged his murder. Werner Herzog said he partly modelled Aguirre in Aguirre, the Wrath of God on Okello.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Okello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A month after the Sultanate of Zanzibar became independent, on 12 January 1964 an uprising led by the Ugandan-born <b>John Okello</b> and supporters of the Afro-Shirazi Party overthrew Sultan Jamshid bin Abdullah and the Arab-dominated government.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-zanzibar">People's Republic of Zanzibar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lango sub-region, in what is now <a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=2.3,33&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Lango sub-region on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Okello"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5256 4803h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5256 4803h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=2.3,33&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
